%I A400362 #8 Oct 04 2026 15:02:50
%S A400362 2,3,4,5,3,6,2,7,5,9,4,10,5,3,8,6,9,2,7,10,16,6,8,18,12,3,4,6,10,8,22,
%T A400362 7,9,16,12,8,10,13,7,28,9,5,2,15,12,10,16,22,9,11,8,36,14,18,10,12,3,
%U A400362 26,40,11,4,6,28,10,14,9,22,5,46,18,11,13,10,19,16
%N A400362 Smallest base in which the n-th Brazilian number (A125134) is a repdigit.
%C A400362 Let B(n) = A125134(n), the n-th Brazilian number. Then a(n) is the least base b, 1 < b < B(n)-1, such that the base-b representation of B(n) consists of identical digits. Thus a(n) is the smallest base that gives support to the fact that B(n) is Brazilian. Equivalently, this sequence is A059711 restricted to the Brazilian numbers A125134.
%D A400362 J. Grantham and H. Graves, Brazilian primes which are also Sophie Germain primes, Integers 20, No. A32 (2020), 4 pp.
%H A400362 Robert Israel, <a href="/A400362/b400362.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%F A400362 a(n) = A059711(A125134(n)).
%e A400362 a(1) = 2 since A125134(1) = 7 = 111_2.
%e A400362 a(5) = 3 since A125134(5) = 13 = 111_3.
%e A400362 a(13) = 5 since A125134(13) = 24 = 44_5 = 33_7 = 22_11, and 5 is the smallest base in which 24 is a repdigit.
%p A400362 g:= proc(k) local b;
%p A400362   for b from 2 to k-2 do
%p A400362     if nops(convert(convert(k,base,b),set))=1 then return b fi
%p A400362   od;
%p A400362   NULL
%p A400362 end proc:
%p A400362 map(g, [$1..200]); # _Robert Israel_, Oct 01 2026
%t A400362 Take[DeleteMissing[Table[SelectFirst[Range[2, n - 2], SameQ @@ IntegerDigits[n, #] &],{n, 4, 100}]],20]
%Y A400362 Cf. A125134, A059711.
%K A400362 nonn,base,easy,new
%O A400362 1,1
%A A400362 _Dario T. de Castro_, Sep 24 2026
